Recommended Study Path:

Building Modern Web Applications with Go (Golang)

Purpose: Foundational course to get you comfortable with Go syntax and concepts.
Why First? This course lays the groundwork by covering Go's core features for web development, which is essential before moving on to more specialized topics.
Focus: Web frameworks, handling requests/responses, and Go project setup.


Building Web Applications with Go - Intermediate Level

Purpose: Enhances your foundational skills with more complex web app logic and design.
Why Next? Strengthen your grasp on Go by dealing with real-world scenarios and scaling web applications. It’s a natural progression after the beginner course.
Focus: APIs, error handling, database interaction, and testing.


Working with Microservices in Go (Golang)

Purpose: Essential for DevOps since microservices are widely used in cloud-based environments.
Why Here? After gaining web development experience, you can dive into microservices, which aligns closely with modern cloud practices.
Focus: Creating, deploying, and managing Go microservices in production environments.


Working with Concurrency in Go (Golang)

Purpose: Master Go's concurrency features (goroutines, channels), crucial for building efficient systems.
Why After Microservices? Concurrency is vital for building scalable applications and services. It will help you optimize microservices for better performance.
Focus: Goroutines, channels, synchronization patterns, and concurrency pitfalls.


Working with WebSockets in Go (Golang)

Purpose: Useful for real-time communication features (like live dashboards, chats, notifications).
Why Now? With experience in building web apps and microservices, you'll appreciate adding real-time communication capabilities using WebSockets.
Focus: WebSocket protocol, message handling, and real-time data flows.


Backend Master Class [Golang + Postgres + Kubernetes + gRPC]

Purpose: This comprehensive course ties Go with other DevOps tools (Postgres, Kubernetes, gRPC).
Why Late in the Sequence? It combines multiple technologies, so it’s better to tackle it when you’re comfortable with Go basics and microservices.
Focus: End-to-end backend development, cloud-native principles, Kubernetes orchestration, and gRPC for communication.


Working with Design Patterns in Go (Golang)

Purpose: Learn reusable design patterns to improve the architecture and scalability of your applications.
Why Last? Design patterns make the most sense when you have solid Go programming experience. They’re often the "polish" to ensure best practices in your code.
Focus: Singleton, Factory, Observer, and other patterns to write clean, maintainable code.


